[p1] Him also and he spoke about him. Not indeed also if the Father would be glorified through him, and since it has been laid down 'through faith a propitiation,' and it has become also 'of the saints a minister' because of the human [nature], as a spiritual sacrifice the prayer of each of the saints he performs; [p2] for through him is our 'Amen'; [p3] and he would not think otherwise than that it is necessary both to worship and to glorify-with the one having been born from among ourselves and from the upper multitude of the holy spirits. [p4] And Paul the Lawgiver said once 'When he shall again bring in the firstborn into the inhabited world,' he says 'And let all angels worship him' 'of God'; and even to whom of the existing things is it obscure as he is Only-begotten according to what one would think God? For alone from alone has been born of the Father; [p5] and since each has become for himself in the [ones] among us as brothers in many [ways], then he himself has been laid down as firstborn; [p6] and regarding being Only-begotten as God, he has also been used as firstborn because of the human [nature]. [p7] And the one confirming us with you into Christ etc. [p8] God and Father confirms us into Christ, having strengthened a right and unshakeable faith for the souls of all, persuading to think how he is by nature and in truth God, even if in the [one] among us he was seen in form, having been born according to flesh from a woman the [one] above all creation.

[p1] And indeed our Lord Jesus Christ himself, while Peter was confessing the faith, saying clearly 'You are the Christ, the Son of the living God,' reflected back saying 'Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah, because flesh and blood did not reveal to you, but my Father who is in the heavens.' Since indeed you have been made to experience the mystery, it is fitting that [it be] a kind of initiation from above and from the Father; [p2] for indeed He himself confirms us into Christ, the [one] who having sealed us and anointed us as God, also gave the pledge of the Spirit. [p3] And it would not be inconsistent for us also because of these things, that the Son had not become 'yes and no'; but rather the true God, and upon all good things 'in him [is] the yes.' For it is said of God who seals and anoints us and gives us the pledge of the Spirit, that Christ would be again the [one] fulfilling these things in us, not as a subordinate, nor indeed as an alien anointing and sealing us with Spirit, but by himself and by that of the Father; [p4] for in both is the Holy Spirit, through the identity of essence, not divided, but as proceeding from the Father through the Son upon the creation; [p5] for He breathed into the holy apostles saying 'Receive the Holy Spirit'; and through him and in him, according to the likeness, we were sealed with the divine and the intelligible.

For he said indeed the divine apostle to the Galatians writing 'My children, whom again I suffer pains, until when Christ is formed in you;' if indeed into Christ being formed, the divine in themselves we enrich [by] likeness, he himself therefore is the image of God and Father and the precise representation, and not just as we according to participation of sanctification are called toward similarity, but by nature and essentially;
